 "indicator": {
  "definition": {
   "definition": "Treasury bills are short-term money-market instruments issued by the Government of India and sold through auctions conducted by the RBI in three tenors — 91-day, 182-day and 364-day. They are zero-coupon securities issued at a discount and redeemed at face value, so the return is the difference between face value and issue price; the cut-off (primary) yield is set in a multiple-price auction, normally held on Wednesdays on the RBI's NDS platform with T+1 settlement. Cash Management Bills, introduced in 2010 by the Government in consultation with the RBI, are discounted instruments with the generic character of T-bills issued to bridge temporary cash-flow mismatches of the Government, with maturities shorter than 91 days.",
   "source_name": "Reserve Bank of India — Government Securities Market in India: A Primer (FAQs)",
   "source_url": "https://rbi.org.in/scripts/FS_FAQs.aspx?Id=79",
   "term": "Treasury bills (91-day, 182-day, 364-day) and Cash Management Bills"
